# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# A TLS session that goes away while a BDAT chunk is still being read leaves the
# server holding readers, and a transfer buffer, belonging to a session that has
# ceased to exist.  Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into
# the released buffer or read the rest of the connection in clear, on the socket
# the peer negotiated encryption for.
#
# So the reply expected below is the lost-connection one: no further byte of the
# chunk may be taken from the plain socket, no message may be accepted, and the
# daemon must not be left waiting out the receive timeout for a chunk that will
# never be finished.  The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421"
# because that timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be
# satisfied by the very behaviour this test exists to reject.
#
# Note for running this by hand: the daemon under test has to be the GnuTLS
# build, which is what runtest uses at both ends when no --tls option is given.
# "--tls gnutls" chooses the library for the client and puts the other build on
# the server side, where none of this applies.  Test numbers spanning more than
# one script directory have to be asked for as a range, or one batch per
# directory.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 85 declared, 84 sent, so one byte is still outstanding when
# the session goes.  The ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF the client
# would otherwise add, so the payload is exactly the text after it:
#
#     To: Susan@random.com CRLF      22
#     From: Sam@random.com CRLF      22   (44)
#     CRLF, ending the header block   2   (46)
#     body-start-                    11   (57)
#     27 x A                         27   (84)
#
# Both halves of that shape are load-bearing.  The header block has to be
# complete, so that reception is past the header lines and into the body: that is
# where the end of a chunk is handled by pushing a byte back through the saved
# readers, which writes into the transfer buffer.  And the chunk has to be short
# by exactly one byte, that byte then being offered in clear once the session has
# gone - a server that still reads the plain socket takes it, finishes the chunk,
# and performs the push-back.  Leave either half out and the reader never gets
# that far, so a build carrying the fault would look every bit as healthy as one
# without it.
#
# The connection is not read again after the reply.  The byte offered in clear is
# deliberately never consumed, so the close that follows carries a reset, and a
# further read would report that reset rather than end of file.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 85 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com\r\nFrom: Sam@random.com\r\n\r\nbody-start-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
stoptls
>>> X
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
no_msglog_check
